{"requirements":[{"id":"baseline","requirement":"Establish a usable integrity baseline covering /etc on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"AIDE was configured with an /etc-only SHA-256 and metadata policy, initialized with 1,950 entries, and produced a clean comparison. The configuration, unchanged database, active timer, and clean check were directly verified after a reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1e37a0f26e93436b","cmd-3a0977fe1f0049d8","cmd-5bd5a07be4344ecc","cmd-2f30bd6f61b04e53","cmd-43c1fb3bad644f02"]},{"id":"change-detection-reporting","requirement":"Allow later changes under /etc to be identified and reported.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A temporary file added under /etc caused a non-clean AIDE result that explicitly reported one added entry and named the file. After cleanup, the scheduled service completed successfully and wrote a clean report to the AIDE log.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b6f3455ed1034573","cmd-5bd5a07be4344ecc","cmd-566416aeb6344c0e"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.72,"summary":"The task-related mutations—installing AIDE, configuring its policy and timer, initializing the database, performing a bounded detection probe, removing that probe, and rebooting for the required protocol—had clear purposes. The probe was fully removed and the baseline returned to clean state. One retained, unnecessary side effect is evident: the AIDE installation also installed and activated Postfix, which introduced SMTP listeners on all IPv4 and IPv6 interfaces at port 25; this exposure was not needed for the demonstrated log-based reporting and was not removed or restricted. No failed services or package inconsistency remained. No unrelated troubleshooting mutations were identified.","evidence_ids":["cmd-733b4b649eb7423d","cmd-dda7b1f8712146e4","cmd-b6f3455ed1034573","cmd-5bd5a07be4344ecc","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The task outcomes are satisfied. Node1 has a clean, persistent /etc integrity baseline, an actual added-file test was detected and reported, and scheduled checks produce reports. The provider restart protocol was also complete: it captured a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged the restart, observed a changed boot ID, and verified recovered configuration, database, timer, and service behavior; the lifecycle boot observations independently agree.","limitations":["The scheduled reporting path was exercised only in the clean state; changed-state reporting was demonstrated through a direct AIDE check rather than through the timer.","The scheduled service journal warned that mail delivery was unavailable in its execution context. File-based reporting was directly verified, but email delivery was not.","Installing AIDE caused Postfix to listen publicly on port 25, reducing operational hygiene despite otherwise healthy final service and package state."]}
